\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{A three-by-three torus calculation}
\date{2026-11-01}

\p{For #{N_1=N_2=3}, there are 9 vertices, 18 edges and 9
plaquettes. The [plaquette constraint](fdip-006Q) permits only an even
number #{k} of negative plaquettes. There are #{\binom9k} such
patterns and #{2^{10}=1024} edge configurations for each pattern.
The resulting numbers of edge configurations are
##{\begin{array}{c|rrrrr}
k&0&2&4&6&8\\\hline
\text{count}&1024&36864&129024&86016&9216.
\end{array}}
They sum to #{262144=2^{18}}, the number of all edge assignments.}

\p{Since #{\sum_pU_p=9-2k}, the partition polynomial is
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal Z(\beta)=1024\bigl(&\ue^{9\beta}+36\ue^{5\beta}+126\ue^\beta\\
                         &+84\ue^{-3\beta}+9\ue^{-7\beta}\bigr),\\
Z_W(\beta)&=\ue^{-9\beta}\mathcal Z(\beta).
\end{aligned}}
At #{\beta=0}, this again gives #{2^{18}}. The factor #{\ue^{-9\beta}}
is necessary for the Wilson-action partition function even though it
cancels from every normalized observable.}

\p{For #{\tanh\beta=1/2}, the [exact loop formula](fdip-006R) gives
##{\begin{aligned}
\langle W(C_1)\rangle_W
 &=\frac{(1/2)+(1/2)^8}{1+(1/2)^9}=\frac{86}{171},\\
\langle W(C_2)\rangle_W
 &=\frac{(1/2)^2+(1/2)^7}{1+(1/2)^9}=\frac{44}{171}.
\end{aligned}}
Here #{C_1} bounds one square and #{C_2} bounds two adjacent squares,
each a simple disk boundary on this torus. These values include the
finite-volume corrections; they are not yet #{t} and #{t^2}.}
